      "message": "\u003e \u003e \u003e Probably the reason it doesn\u0027t break/error out is because it just accepts it as the version string [1] while 3.9 just passes it in [2]\n\u003e \u003e \u003e \n\u003e \u003e \n\u003e \u003e Right, I was about to reply with the same thing. I think the only risk might be if the original find_package used REQUIRED too. Let me try that.\n\u003e \u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e We get find_package(Threads REQUIRED REQUIRED) in that case, which still works, though it might be a little risky.\n\u003e \n\nEven later versions of the implementation operate in the same way, so it looks like this should be safe.\n\n\u003e \u003e \u003e [1] https://github.com/Kitware/CMake/blob/v3.7.0/Modules/CMakeFindDependencyMacro.cmake#L30\n\u003e \u003e \u003e [2] https://github.com/Kitware/CMake/blob/859241d2bbaae83f06c44bc21ab0dbd38725a3fc/Modules/CMakeFindDependencyMacro.cmake#L47",
